PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The consensus remains solidly anchored on Choice A ("Heat capacity change is assumed to be zero"), which directly reflects the literature on nearest-neighbor base-pair (NNBP) parameterization and single-molecule unzipping. In bulk thermal melting, transitions occur at elevated, sequence-dependent temperatures ($T_m$), requiring the assumption that $\Delta C_p^\circ = 0$ to extrapolate enthalpy and entropy to physiological temperatures ($37\ ^\circ\text{C}$). The distractor architecture (Choices D and E regarding temperature control and thermal fluctuations) reinforces that the question tests the inability to capture temperature dependence under ideal conditions. While Choice C describes a generic limitation of ensemble measurements, NNBP model training duplexes were specifically engineered to minimize conformational heterogeneity, leaving Choice A as the definitive target limitation.
}

